{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 26, :comments ["<b>And HaShem was wrathful, etc., and He did not listen, etc.</b> He repeated, saying ״וַיִּתְעַבֵּר״ and ״לֹא שָׁמַע,״ and He interrupted with the word <b>לְמַעַנְכֶם</b> between the statement ״וַיִּתְעַבֵּר״ and the statement ״וְלֹא שָׁמַע,״ for he should have said ״וַיִּתְעַבֵּר HaShem בִּי וְלֹא שָׁמַע אֵלַי לְמַעַנְכֶם.״ Also, he said the word <b>לְמַעַנְכֶם</b> after having already said above (Deuteronomy 1:37) <b>גַּם בִּי הִתְאַנַּף HaShem בִּגְלַלְכֶם.</b> It will be explained according to what we explained concerning his saying ״בָּעֵת הַהִיא לֵאמֹר,״ that he intended to pray for himself and also for Israel. Therefore, corresponding to what he intended in his prayer for Israel, he said <b>וַיִּתְעַבֵּר HaShem בִּי לְמַעַנְכֶם</b> — meaning, for the sake of what concerns you. And corresponding to what he prayed for himself, he said <b>וְלֹא שָׁמַע אֵלַי</b> — meaning, concerning what concerns me. And in this he did not use a term of wrath, for Moses' petition for himself is the petition of a wise man with a claim that is heard, as we wrote above — unlike Israel, who provoked HaShem and incurred annihilation, in our many sins; therefore he said ״וַיִּתְעַבֵּר.״" "<b>Or</b> it may intend in this manner: <b>וַיִּתְעַבֵּר HaShem בִּי</b> to die outside the land <b>לְמַעַנְכֶם</b>, for the known reason, so that they may stand with him in the world to come, as they of blessed memory said. And therefore he was precise in saying <b>וַיִּתְעַבֵּר</b> — a term of wrath, after the manner of his saying (Zephaniah 1:15) ״יוֹם עֶבְרָה הַיּוֹם הַהוּא״ — which is the day of death. And his saying <b>וְלֹא שָׁמַע אֵלָי</b> — that HaShem did not want him to enter the land at all, even on condition that he leave it afterward and be buried in that place where the dead of the desert were buried, as we explained above concerning the statement ״אֶעְבְּרָה נָא.״" "<b>And He said, etc., רַב לָךְ, etc., do not continue.</b> He repeated, saying ״רַב לָךְ אַל תּוֹסֶף״ — he came to respond to two matters that appear in Moses' prayer. Corresponding to what he prayed for himself, He said to him <b>רַב לָךְ</b>; He hinted to him in the word ״רַב״ that the entire reason for Moses' prayer to enter the land was in order to fulfill the commandments dependent on the land. Therefore He answered him that he does not need this, for he has abundant goodness that HaShem will give him — and that is His saying ״רַב לָךְ״ — and you do not need any additional merit. And corresponding to what he was praying for Israel, He silenced him with a rebuke and said to him ״אַל תּוֹסֶף דַּבֵּר,״ etc." "<b>Or</b> he intended to say in this manner: for every merit that will come to those who enter the land from the commandments dependent on the land, you have a share in all of them. And what is this share? That you are the one who commands them to perform them — and that is His saying <b>רַב לָךְ</b>, meaning, the mastery over the fulfillment of the commandment belongs to you. And if so, you have a share with each and every one in every act that they do there, and therefore: do not continue to speak about this matter." "<b>Further,</b> the statement <b>רַב לָךְ</b> intended to respond to the claim of ״אֶעְבְּרָה נָּא,״ which we explained to mean that he asked Him to transfer him from the kingship on account of the kingship of Joshua. Therefore He said <b>רַב לָךְ</b> — meaning, the greatness and the authority are designated and reserved for you as long as you are in existence, and it is impossible to give the greatness to your disciple during your lifetime. From this it follows there is no remedy for the reason that ״one kingship does not touch its fellow״ etc. (Berakhot 48b). And according to this approach, the statement ״אַל תּוֹסֶף״ is needed for itself and is not superfluous." "<b>Further</b>, it appears to explain on this path: ״רַב לָךְ״ on its own, and the saying <b>do not add</b> continues with what follows it, namely the statement ״עֲלֵה רֹאשׁ הַפִּסְגָּה״ etc. The meaning is that because HaShem said to him ״עֲלֵה רֹאשׁ״ etc., which indicated that half of his prayer had been accepted — for he had said ״וְאֶרְאֶה אֶת הָאָרֶץ הַטּוֹבָה״ — HaShem was concerned that Moses would add and pour out further supplication. And this is for you a sign that HaShem has heard my voice in my supplication. Therefore He said to him ״אַל תּוֹסֶף״ etc. <b>in this matter</b> — meaning, in this matter that I am speaking. And this is what He concluded to say: <b>כִּי לֹא תַעֲבֹר</b> — meaning, this matter is decided and absolute."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:15:09.289283052Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}